<h2>Prepared choice and supported strategies</h2>
<p>The forced-invariant fork is controller authority: (a) a separately configured ceiling, (b) an EWMA-owned ceiling, or (c) a learned ceiling plus weights and parallelism. <strong>Recommend (a)</strong>. Feedback that can enlarge its own constraint cannot guarantee the operator's resource bound. Adding an independent clamp to either alternative is composition with (a), not rejection of the algorithm.</p>
<p>Fixed targets and explicitly enabled adaptive estimators are compatible configuration strategies beneath an independent operator envelope. The <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#delivery-decider-under-fixed-settings">fixed-settings decider rule</a> makes fixed values final and permits automatic fields only inside sibling bounds, initially in shadow. The <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#operator-override-settings-layer">operator override settings layer</a> extends the earlier committed-settings source: resolve standard, platform, tool, environment and then operator overrides, logging the effective source; only the operator authorizes writes and loosening requires confirmation. Resolve these settings first, then bound any automatic target by the independent envelope and invariants. The delivery decider remains deterministic in v1 and only orders heavy-work priority; learned heavy-target estimation is a proposed extension. Keep the existing target and resource gates while collecting comparative evidence. A lower target drains existing work naturally. Missing or partial required inputs cannot expand capacity; defer visibly when the resource gate holds. Current legacy fallback paths do not establish a universal fail-closed guarantee.</p>
